3. Modern Block Ciphers
3.1 DES – Data Encryption Standard
DES is a symmetric-key block cipher that encrypts data in fixed-size blocks.

Important specifications:

Parameter	DES
Block size	64 bits
Key size	64 bits including 8 parity bits
Effective key size	56 bits
Number of rounds	16
Structure	Feistel network
Type	Symmetric block cipher
Working of DES:

Plaintext of 64 bits is taken.
Initial Permutation (IP) is applied.
Data is divided into two 32-bit halves: L and R.
Sixteen Feistel rounds are performed.
Final permutation (inverse IP) is applied.
A 64-bit ciphertext is generated.
What happens in each round?

The right half is expanded from 32 bits to 48 bits.

It is XORed with a 48-bit round key.

The result passes through S-boxes, reducing it to 32 bits.

A permutation is applied, and the result is combined with the left half.

Why is DES insecure today?

Because its effective key length is only 56 bits, making brute-force attacks feasible with modern computing power.

3.2 AES – Advanced Encryption Standard
AES is a modern symmetric-key block cipher used for secure data encryption.

Important specifications:

Parameter	AES
Block size	128 bits
Key size	128, 192, 256 bits
Rounds	10, 12, 14
Structure	Substitution-Permutation Network
Type	Symmetric block cipher
AES variants:

Algorithm	Key size	Rounds
AES-128	128 bits	10
AES-192	192 bits	12
AES-256	256 bits	14
AES working:

Key Expansion – generates round keys.
Initial AddRoundKey – combines plaintext with the first round key.
Main rounds – apply four operations.
Final round – applies three operations, omitting MixColumns.
Four main operations:

SubBytes: Replaces each byte using an S-box.
ShiftRows: Shifts rows of the state matrix.
MixColumns: Mixes data within each column.
AddRoundKey: XORs the state with a round key.
Advantages:

Strong security.
Efficient in hardware and software.
Supports multiple key sizes.
Widely used in modern applications.
DES vs AES
DES	AES
64-bit block	128-bit block
56-bit effective key	128/192/256-bit key
16 rounds	10/12/14 rounds
Feistel network	Substitution-Permutation Network
Insecure against modern brute force	Considered secure when properly implemented
Older standard	Modern standard
Important viva questions:

Why is AES better than DES? Because AES uses larger keys, a larger block size, and a stronger modern design.
Is AES symmetric or asymmetric? Symmetric.
Does AES use a Feistel structure? No.
Does DES use S-boxes? Yes.
What is the block size of AES? 128 bits.
What is the effective key size of DES? 56 bits.
