10. Access Control in Operating Systems
Access control is the process of determining which users or processes can access specific resources and what operations they are permitted to perform.

Example:

An administrator can create and delete users, while a normal user can only access permitted files.

Three access control models are in your syllabus.

10.1 Discretionary Access Control (DAC)
DAC is an access control model in which the owner of a resource decides who can access it.

Example:

A user creates a folder and gives read permission to another user.

Features:

Resource owner controls permissions.
Flexible.
Permissions can be shared or changed.
Common in traditional file systems.
10.2 Mandatory Access Control (MAC)
MAC is an access control model in which access is controlled by a central security policy based on security labels and user clearance levels.

Example:

A military system may classify information as:

Confidential.
Secret.
Top Secret.
A user can access information only if the security policy permits it.

Features:

Strict access control.
Centrally managed.
Users cannot freely change security permissions.
Common in highly secure environments.
10.3 Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)
RBAC grants permissions based on the user's assigned role.

Example:

In a hospital:

Doctor – access patient medical records.
Receptionist – access appointment details.
Administrator – manage system users.
Features:

Permissions are assigned to roles.
Users are assigned roles.
Simplifies permission management.
Useful in organizations.
