Often requires user interaction	Can occur even when user enters the correct URL
Commonly uses social engineering	Often uses DNS or host configuration manipulation
1.6 Dictionary Attack
A dictionary attack is a password-cracking technique in which an attacker tries passwords from a predefined list of commonly used words and password combinations.

Example:

An attacker tries:

password123
admin123
welcome123
qwerty
Prevention:

Strong passwords.
Password length requirements.
MFA.
Login rate limiting.
Account lockout policies.
Secure password hashing using Argon2id, scrypt, or bcrypt with appropriate parameters.
