17. Why is Diffie-Hellman used?

To establish a shared secret over an insecure communication channel.

18. Can Diffie-Hellman be attacked?

Yes, especially through a man-in-the-middle attack if the communicating parties are not authenticated.

19. Why is ECC preferred for resource-constrained devices?

It provides comparable classical security with smaller key sizes and lower bandwidth requirements.

C. Authentication and Access Control
20. What is the difference between MAC and a digital signature?

MAC uses a shared secret key, whereas a digital signature uses a private-public key pair.

21. Does SHA-512 provide encryption?

No, it generates a 512-bit hash digest.

22. Why is MD5 not recommended?

Because it is vulnerable to practical collision attacks.

23. What is the difference between Kerberos and PKI?

Kerberos provides ticket-based authentication using a trusted KDC, while PKI manages public-key certificates and trust relationships.

24. What is a digital certificate?

An electronic document that binds an identity to a public key and is digitally signed by a trusted issuer.

25. What is the purpose of a nonce?

To provide freshness and help prevent replay attacks.

D. Network and Application Security
26. What is the difference between a firewall and an IDS?

A firewall filters network traffic, while an IDS monitors traffic and detects suspicious activities.

27. What is the difference between IDS and IPS?

IDS detects and alerts; IPS detects and blocks.

28. What is the difference between IPsec and TLS?

IPsec protects IP communication at the network layer, while TLS protects communication between applications over a transport connection.

29. What is the difference between HTTP and HTTPS?

HTTP transfers web data without TLS protection, while HTTPS protects HTTP communication using TLS.

30. Why is TLS preferred over SSL?

SSL is outdated and insecure; TLS provides modern cryptographic security.

31. What is the difference between AH and ESP?

AH provides integrity and authentication but not encryption. ESP can provide confidentiality and also integrity/authentication.

32. What is the purpose of a honeypot?

To attract attackers and observe their behaviour.

E. Cybersecurity and Forensics
33. What is the difference between cybercrime and cyberattack?

Cybercrime is an illegal activity involving digital systems. A cyberattack is an intentional attempt to compromise a system; not every security test or attack simulation is a crime.

34. What is digital evidence?

Information stored or transmitted electronically that may be used in an investigation.

35. Why is chain of custody important?

It documents evidence handling and helps establish that evidence was not improperly altered or substituted.

36. Why is hashing important in digital forensics?

It helps verify whether evidence has changed.

37. What is the difference between logical and physical acquisition?

Logical acquisition extracts data through supported interfaces and file structures; physical acquisition attempts to capture storage at a lower, bit-level representation.

38. What is the difference between active and passive reconnaissance?

Passive reconnaissance uses publicly available information without direct target interaction, whereas active reconnaissance directly probes the target.

39. What is tokenization in e-commerce?

Replacing sensitive payment information with a substitute token.

40. What is the purpose of forensic imaging?

To create a forensic copy for investigation while preserving the original evidence.

